'''travail<sup>1</sup>''' (trav′āl), ''v. i.'' [As with the noun, an earlier form of ''travel'', now differentiated in a particular use (def. 2): see ''travel, v.''] '''1'''†. To labor; toil; travel: same as ''travel'', 1.—'''2'''. To labor in childbed; suffer the pangs of childbirth; be parturient. [Archaic.]

{{smaller block|No<sup>a</sup>. that relyques of the stones of the place there our Lady was borne is remedy and consolacion to women that ''trauayll'' of childe. {{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 30.}}}}

{{smaller block|And when she heard the tidings . . . she bowed herself and ''travailed;'' for her pains came upon her. {{right|1 Sam. iv. 19.}}}}

{{smaller block|Queen Jeanie ''travel'd'' six weeks and more,
Till women and midwives had quite gi'en her o'er.
{{right|''Queen Jeanie'' (Child's Ballads, VII. 75).}}}}

'''travail<sup>2</sup>''' (F. pron. tra-vay′), ''n.''; F. pl. ''travaux'' (tra-vō′). [< F. ''travail'', a brake, trave, < ML. *''trabaculum'' (also, after Rom., ''trabale'', ''travallum''), a brake, shackle: see ''travel, n.''] A means of transportation, commonly used by North American Indians and voyageurs of the north

readings.c2s3:
and northwest, for the conveyance of goods or of sick or wounded persons. {{smaller|It consists of a rude litter made of two lodge-poles about 16 feet long, having one end of each pole attached on each side to a pack-saddle, the other end trailing on the ground. A kind of sack or bag is then made by lashing canvas or lodge-skins to the cross-bars, for the reception of the goods or the sick or wounded person. Also called ''travois'', ''travee''.}}

{{smaller block|In a month "Richard 's himself again," ready to fly over the grassy sward with his savage master, or to drag the ''travaux'' and pack the buxom squaw.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXVII. 339.}}}}

'''travailer†''', ''n.'' An old spelling of ''traveler''.

'''travailous†''', ''a.'' See ''travelous''.

'''travale''' (tra-val′), ''n.'' In ''tambourine-playing'', an effect produced by rubbing the wetted finger across the head of the instrument. The ''double travale'' is simply the same effect made twice as rapidly as usual.

'''trave''' (trāv), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''treve''; < ME. ''trave'', < OF. ''traf'', ''tref'', ''trief'', a cross-beam, a brake, shackle, = Pr. ''trau'' = Sp. ''trabe'', ''traba'' = Pg. ''trava'', ''trave'' = It. ''trave'', < L. ''trabs'', ''trabis'', a beam. Hence ult. ''travail''<sup>1</sup>, ''travel''.] 1. A cross-beam; a beam or timber-work crossing a building.

{{smaller block|The Ceilings and ''Traves'' are, after the Turkish manner, richly Painted and Guilded.
{{right|''Maundrell'', Aleppo to Jerusalem, p. 125.}}}}

2. A kind of shackle for a horse that is being taught to amble or pace.

{{smaller block|She sproong as a colt doth in the ''trave''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller's Tale, l. 96.}}}}

Also ''travis''.

